.H 2 "Charsets and Transfer Encodings"
.P
Conversions read and write the encodings of one registry, which
holds two kinds of entry.
A
.I charset
maps bytes to Unicode code points and back.
A
.I "transfer encoding"
maps bytes to ASCII text and back, with no characters involved:
hex, base64 and the crypt alphabets.
The registry holds more than 100 charsets: the Unicode encodings,
the Windows code pages, published tables from standards bodies
and vendors, and the multi-byte charsets of Chinese, Japanese and
Korean (together, CJK).
This section describes them by family; Appendix C lists every
name, with the tokens and the
.B charsets()
groups.
.SK
.H 3 "Charsets"
.P
The registry holds the charsets below and two aliases:
.B gbk
is
.B cp936
and
.B big5
is
.BR cp950 .
Each family reads one table, from the source that
.B "hx \-V"
names.
.na
