.TE
.ad
.TB "Published Single-Byte Tables"
.P
To
.I decode
a byte, hx looks it up in the table and reads the character the
table gives; to
.I encode
a character, it looks the character up in the other direction and
writes the byte.
Most entries give one byte one code point.
Some give a byte a
.I sequence
of code points, because the character the byte shows has no single
code point in Unicode: in
.BR mac_hebrew ,
0x81 is the Yiddish ligature yod yod patah, U+05F2 followed by
U+05B7.
.P
Unicode leaves the code points U+E000\(enU+F8FF, the
.IR "private-use area" ,
without characters, for vendors and applications to assign as
they choose.
Apple's tables use some of them as
.IR "variant tags" :
a tag after a character names one form of it.
In
.BR adobe_symbol ,
0xD2 is \(lq\(rg\(rq (U+00AE) and 0xE2 is its sans-serif form,
U+00AE followed by the tag U+F87F.
A byte decodes to its whole sequence, tags included.
.P
Some entries also carry a
.IR hint ,
written in angle brackets, which says how the byte is displayed
and is not a character.
Apple's Arabic and Hebrew tables hold two copies of the space and
of some punctuation: one marked
.B <LR>
for left-to-right text and one marked
.B <RL>
for right-to-left text.
L2/19-025's Atari table marks with
.B <RV>
the bytes that show a character in reverse video, light on dark.
hx drops the hint, so the two bytes of a pair decode to the same
code point.
.P
The encoder writes the longest sequence the table has a byte for,
so U+00AE followed by U+F87F encodes to 0xE2 and U+00AE alone to
0xD2.
Where several bytes carry one sequence, it takes the byte without
an
.B <RL>
or
.B <RV>
hint, then the lowest byte.
MacArabic has the space at 0x20 and, with
.BR <RL> ,
at 0xA0: both decode to U+0020, and U+0020 encodes to 0x20.
.P
The bytes 0x00\(en0x1F and 0x7F are the ASCII control characters:
tab, line feed, carriage return and others that are not printed.
0x00\(en0x1F are the
.IR "C0 controls" ,
and 0x7F is DEL.
Apple's tables and JIS X 0201 leave them out, and they decode to
themselves.
.P
A byte that the table does not define, such as 0xA5 in ISO 8859-3,
is
.IR ill-formed :
it stands for no character.
.B convert()
takes tokens that select how an ill-formed byte is treated
(Section 4.3.1), and the predicate
.B valid()
is true only when every byte is defined (Section 4.7.2).
A table that defines every byte, such as
.B latin1
or
.BR koi8r ,
accepts these tokens too: they cannot change its decoding, so the
compiler drops them, and the instruction is the same as with none.
.P
JIS X 0201 holds the Japanese katakana in single bytes,
0xA1\(en0xDF.
Unicode writes these as the
.I halfwidth
katakana, U+FF61\(enU+FF9F, separate from the ordinary katakana
from U+30A1.
.B jis_x0201
follows JIS0201.TXT and decodes 0xB1 to the halfwidth U+FF71;
glibc's JIS_X0201 decodes it to the ordinary U+30A2 and is not
used.
.P
The IBM PC showed a picture, a
.IR glyph ,
for each control byte: 0x01 is a smiling face (U+263A).
IBMGRAPH.TXT gives those pictures for CP864, the Arabic page, but
Windows does not use them: even with
.B MB_USEGLYPHCHARS
it changes only 0x7F (Section 4.3.1).
.B cp864_ibmgraph
is CP864 as the IBM PC showed it: Microsoft's CP864.TXT, with
0x01\(en0x1F and 0x7F from IBMGRAPH.TXT, 0x25 as
.B %
as Windows has it, and 0xA6, 0xA7 and 0xFF, which CP864.TXT does
not define, ill-formed.
.P
A
.I "multi-byte charset"
writes a character as one or more bytes.
In the double-byte charsets (Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, EUC-KR, GB 2312,
GBK and Big5), a character outside ASCII is two bytes: a
.IR "lead byte" ,
from a range that the charset reserves for it, and a
.I "trail byte"
after it.
The characters that share a lead byte make up one
.I row
of the table.
GB 18030 also has four-byte codes, so that it can write every
Unicode character.
EUC-JP has two
.IR "single shifts" :
0x8E is followed by one JIS X 0201 katakana byte, and 0x8F by a
two-byte JIS X 0212 character.
ISO-2022-JP has no lead bytes.
An
.I "escape sequence"
switches its character set: ESC (0x1B) and two bytes after it
select ASCII
.RB ( "ESC ( B" ),
JIS-Roman
.RB ( "ESC ( J" )
or the two-byte JIS X 0208
.RB ( "ESC $ @" ,
.BR "ESC $ B" ),
and the text must end in ASCII.
.P
The WHATWG
.I "Encoding Standard"
is the specification that web browsers follow for these charsets.
It numbers the codes of each table with an index, the
.IR pointer ,
and it defines which bytes form one error.
The standard and
.B _web
multi-byte charsets follow that error rule; the
.BI cp NNN
double-byte pages follow Windows instead (Section 4.5.2).
A lead byte is any byte in the lead range, even when its row holds
no character.
If the byte after a lead is not ASCII and the two make no
character, the two bytes are one ill-formed unit; if that byte is
ASCII, the lead alone is the unit, and the ASCII byte is read again
as a character of its own.
Input that ends inside a GB 18030 four-byte code is one unit.
In ISO-2022-JP, an escape sequence that is not one of the four is
ill-formed at the ESC alone, and the bytes after it are read again
as text, as the Standard's decoder does: 1B 28 41 is one
ill-formed unit, then \(lq(\(rq and \(lqA\(rq.
.BL
.LI
GB 2312 is the original Simplified Chinese standard, and GB 18030
contains it.
Two of its codes have two mappings in use: glibc's GB2312 maps
0xA1A4 to U+30FB (katakana middle dot) and 0xA1AA to U+2015
(horizontal bar), and GB 18030 maps them to U+00B7 (middle dot)
and U+2014 (em dash).
.B gb2312
uses the GB 18030 mappings.
.LI
GB 18030-2005 mapped 18 characters that Unicode had not yet
encoded to private-use code points.
GB 18030-2022 maps their two-byte codes to the characters Unicode
now has, and the private-use code points keep only their four-byte
codes.
.B gb18030
is the Encoding Standard's algorithm, except that 0x80, which the
Standard decodes as \[u20AC], is undefined, and 0xA3A0, which it
decodes as U+3000, is the private-use U+E5E5.
Every Unicode
.I "scalar value"
(every code point except the surrogates U+D800\(enU+DFFF) has
exactly one code, and that code decodes back to it.
The four-byte pointers 39420\(en188999 and those above 1237575 have
no character and are ill-formed.
.LI
Big5 is the Traditional Chinese double-byte charset, and
Big5-HKSCS adds the Hong Kong Supplementary Character Set to it.
.B big5_web
is Big5-HKSCS as the Encoding Standard defines it.
Four of its codes decode to two code points, a letter and a
combining mark: 0x8862 is \(lq\[u00CA]\(rq followed by U+0304
(combining macron).
.LI
GBK is the two-byte part of GB 18030, the form that Windows uses
as CP936.
.B gbk_web
has the decoder of
.B gb18030_web
and an encoder that writes \[u20AC] as 0x80 and has no four-byte
codes.
.LI
UHC, Unified Hangul Code, is Microsoft's extension of EUC-KR
(Windows CP949), which adds the 8,822 Hangul syllables that KS X
1001 lacks.
.B euc_kr_web
is UHC.
.LI
EUC, Extended Unix Code, writes its two-byte codes with bytes
0xA1\(en0xFE, so the bytes 0x80\(en0x9F are outside them, apart from
EUC-JP's single shifts.
ISO 6429 assigns those bytes to the
.IR "C1 controls" ,
U+0080\(enU+009F.
The standard EUC forms leave the bytes undefined, as browsers,
Java, Python, Perl and macOS iconv do.
glibc's iconv and IBM's converters (ibm-33722, ibm-970) decode
them as the C1 controls, and that reading is
.B euc_jp_c1
and
.BR euc_kr_c1 .
.B euc_kr_c1
is all of glibc's EUC-KR; IBM's ibm-970 leaves out 0x8E and 0x8F.
.B euc_jp_c1
is all of glibc's EUC-JP except A1C0 and 8FA2B7, which glibc maps
to the fullwidth U+FF3C and U+FF5E, and which keep the readings of
.B euc_jp
(\f(CW\e\fP and \f(CW\(ti\fP).
No decoder reads GB 2312's 0x80\(en0x9F as C1, so
.B gb2312
has no C1 form.
.LI
GSM 03.38 is the 7-bit alphabet of SMS text messages.
Each character is a
.IR septet ,
a 7-bit value, and 0x1B leads a character of the extension table:
0x1B 0x65 is \[u20AC].
.B gsm0338
holds one septet in each byte.
SMS packs eight septets into seven bytes; that
.I "septet packing"
is not part of the charset.
0x09 is \(lq\[u00C7]\(rq (U+00C7), as 3GPP prints it, where
unicode.org's GSM0338.TXT has \(lq\[u00E7]\(rq.
.LI
T.61, the Teletex character set, writes an accented letter as two
bytes: an
.I "accent byte"
from 0xC1\(en0xCF, and the letter after it.
0xC2 0x41 is \(lq\[u00C1]\(rq.
An accent byte followed by a space is the accent alone, as a
spacing character: 0xC2 0x20 is \(lq\[u00B4]\(rq (U+00B4).
.LE
.P
UTF-7 writes Unicode in 7-bit bytes, for mail systems that carry
only ASCII.
RFC 2152 names two sets of characters that stand for themselves:
.I "Set D"
is the letters, the digits and
.ft CW
\(aq ( ) , \- . / : ?
.ft R
and
.I "Set O"
is
.ft CW
! " # $ % & * ; < = > @ [ ] \(ha _ \(ga { | }\fR.
.ft R
Any other character is written as its UTF-16 units in base64
(Section 4.2.2), in a
.I "base64 run"
that begins with
.BR + ;
\f(CW+-\fP is the plus sign itself.
.B utf7
decodes as RFC 2152 defines.
A base64 run ends at
.BR \- ,
which is absorbed, or at any other byte outside the base64
alphabet, which is read as itself.
The run is ill-formed if it holds no whole UTF-16 unit, if 6 or
more bits are left over, if the bits left over are not zero, or if
a surrogate is not paired.
A byte outside Set D, Set O, SP, TAB, CR and LF is ill-formed,
including
.B \(ti
and
.BR \e .
.P
