.TE
.ad
.TB "Code Pages"
.P
Each
.BI cp NNN
table is generated from what Windows 11 does, not from a
published file.
Every byte, and on a double-byte page every lead and trail
pair, was decoded with
.BR MultiByteToWideChar ,
once with no flags and once with
.BR MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS ;
every BMP code point other than a surrogate was encoded with
.BR WideCharToMultiByte ,
once with best fit and once with
.BR WC_NO_BEST_FIT_CHARS ;
and every byte of the 16 OEM pages and 720 was decoded again with
.BR MB_USEGLYPHCHARS .
These tables differ from Microsoft's published code-page files,
chiefly in best fits for the spaces U+2000\(enU+200A, which
the files omit.
.P
Where a standard and a Windows page both exist, they are separate
readings under separate names:
.B iso8859_7
is ISO/IEC 8859-7 and
.B cp28597
is Windows' reading of it,
.B mac_roman
is Apple's table and
.B cp10000
Windows',
.B euc_kr
is KS X 1001 and
.B cp949
is Windows' UHC.
A name ending in
.B _web
is the Encoding Standard's reading, which is what a browser sends.
.na
