.ad
.TB "Strict and Lenient Decoding"
.P
.B QUJ
is null because its last character carries the bits 01 below the
decoded bytes, which no encoder writes;
.B QUI
carries 00 and decodes.
.SK
.H 2 "convert()"
.P
.B convert()
is the conversion instruction itself.
Every function in the table of Section 4.4 is a name for one of
its forms.
.DS
.ft CW
convert(x, from, to)
convert(x, from, to, token, ...)
.ft R
.DE
.P
.I from
and
.I to
are string literals naming two registry entries of the same
kind.
Each further argument is a string literal holding one policy
token or one premap token.
Tokens may be given in any order, and at most one from each group.
.P
A conversion between transfer encodings is lossless: bytes encoded
into hex, base64, base32 or a crypt alphabet decode back to the
same bytes.
A conversion between charsets is not, and it needs care.
The character that a byte stands for depends on the charset, and
the bytes do not say which charset wrote them, so the result is
only as correct as the
.I from
that is named.
A character the target cannot write is lost: it becomes a default
character, a best fit or a null result.
Input that is ill-formed in its charset is handled by policy, not
repaired.
Know the form of the input before writing the conversion: which
charset wrote it, and whether another program changed it after it
was written.
.P
.BR convert() ,
nested and inside loops (Section 4.7), can describe very complex
encodings, such as text that passed through several programs, each
with its own code page.
It is not a cure for every encoding problem.
Apart from a premap or a best fit, it changes how characters are
written, never which characters they are: it does not translate
text from one language or script into another, and it does not
transliterate.
It does not find the encoding of its input; it does what the
expression says.
And it cannot correct bad input.
A wrong conversion that lost nothing, such as UTF-8 read as CP1252,
can be reversed (Section 4.4), but characters that were truncated
or replaced with
.B ?
or U+FFFD are gone, and no conversion restores them.
Each conversion has been tested exhaustively against its source
(Section 4.8), but some inputs cannot be converted, and for those
the result is null (Section 4.3.4).
.P
Between two charsets,
.B convert()
decodes
.I x
from
.I from
into code points, applies the premap, and encodes the code points
into
.IR to .
Any pair is meaningful, including pairs that no named function
covers:
.B convert(pass,\ \(dqcp437\(dq,\ \(dqcp1252\(dq)
re-encodes CP437 text in CP1252.
Between two transfer encodings it decodes
.I from
to bytes, applies the premap to the bytes, and encodes
.IR to ,
so
.B convert(h,\ \(dqhex\(dq,\ \(dqbase64\(dq)
turns a hex digest into its base64 form.
A charset and a transfer encoding cannot be mixed in one call:
a charset reads text and a transfer encoding reads bytes, so the
meaning of the pair would be a guess.
Nest the calls instead:
.DS
.ft CW
convert(convert(pass, "utf8", "utf16le"), "raw", "base64")
.ft R
.DE
.P
.B "hx \-b"
prints the instruction with its operands, for example
.DS
.ft CW
CONVERT    convert utf8->cp437 [bestfit,fail] premap=upper_oem
.ft R
.DE
.H 3 "Policy Tokens"
.P
A policy token says what happens to input the decoder cannot
read, to output the encoder cannot write, and to lone surrogates.
Without a token, the group's default (listed first) applies.
.na
.TS H
