.ad
.TB "Policy Tokens"
.P
An ill-formed unit is one byte of ill-formed UTF-8; in a standard
single-byte charset, a byte the table does not define; in UTF-32, a
four-byte unit that is not a scalar value, or a partial final unit;
in
.BR cesu8 ,
a four-byte form or a lone surrogate; in
.BR utf7 ,
an ill-formed base64 run or a byte outside the RFC 2152 sets; in a
multi-byte charset,
.B gsm0338
or
.BR t61 ,
the bytes that the rules of Section 4.2.1 delimit.
A
.BI cp NNN
source never meets an ill-formed unit: the lenient decode reads
every byte as Windows does, and
.B null
is the strict decode, which rejects what Windows rejects.
.P
A character is unmappable when the target cannot write it: in a
published single-byte table or a multi-byte charset, a code point
the table lacks; in a
.BI cp NNN
target, a UTF-16 unit that the page encodes to its default byte
and that is not itself the character that byte decodes to
(Section 4.5.6); in a Unicode target other than
.BR utf8 ,
or in
.BR lowbyte16 ,
an ill-formed byte kept under
.BR keep ;
and in
.B utf7
and the UTF-32 targets, which cannot hold a surrogate, a lone
surrogate kept under
.BR cesu .
.P
Whether
.B fail
applies is decided twice.
Where the pair can meet an unmappable character under some choice
of tokens,
.B fail
is accepted.
Where the tokens in force, given or default, leave nothing
unmappable,
.B fail
has no effect and is dropped:
.B convert(x,\ \(dqutf8\(dq,\ \(dqutf16le\(dq,\ \(dqnull\(dq,\ \(dqfail\(dq)
is the same instruction as
.BR utf16le(x) ,
because
.B null
leaves nothing to keep.
The default
.B fffd
does the same for a UTF-16 source: it reads a lone surrogate as
U+FFFD, so
.B convert(x,\ \(dqutf16le\(dq,\ \(dqutf7\(dq,\ \(dqfail\(dq)
is the plain instruction, as it is with
.B fffd
written out, and only with
.B cesu
does
.B fail
take effect there.
Where the pair can never meet an unmappable character, as
.B latin1
to
.B utf8
cannot,
.B fail
is a compile-time error.
.na
.TS H
