.ad
.TB "convert() Compile-Time Errors"
.P
A named conversion that does not exist, such as
.B utf8_to_koi8r_bestfit
or
.BR cp01252_to_utf8 ,
is an unknown function (Section 2.7.1).
.H 3 "A Failed Conversion Is Null"
.P
A conversion that cannot be carried out gives the empty string,
with exit status 0.
It never gives an error, and never a partial result: hx has no
other failure channel, and a shortened string could not be told
apart from the conversion of some other input.
The result is null when
.BL
.LI
the source is
.B utf8
under
.B null
and the input is not well-formed UTF-8, as for
.BR utf16le() ,
.B utf16be()
and
.BR utf7() ,
or any other source under
.B null
meets an ill-formed unit (Section 4.3.1);
.LI
a
.BI cp NNN
source under
.B null
meets a byte or pair that the strict Windows decode rejects;
.LI
.B fail
is given and a character is unmappable;
.LI
a strict transfer decoder meets text its encoder cannot produce.
.LE
.P
A hash over a null result hashes the empty string, which looks
like a clean miss.
A type whose definition needs a reading of ill-formed input
states it explicitly: with
.BR zext16() ,
.BR latin1_to_utf8() ,
.B cp1252_to_utf8()
or
.B cp1251_to_utf8()
first, or with the
.B latin1
or
.B fffd
token.
.H 3 "Fusion"
.P
When a program is prepared for execution, two adjacent conversions
are fused into one where the result is provably the same.
Between charsets, \fIA\fP\(->\fIB\fP followed by
\fIB\fP\(->\fIC\fP becomes \fIA\fP\(->\fIC\fP when
.I B
is UTF-8 or UTF-16, at most one of the two has a premap, the
first cannot pass an ill-formed byte or a lone surrogate through
(it is not a source that can meet an ill-formed unit, such as
.B utf8
or
.BR iso8859_3 ,
under
.BR keep ,
or a UTF-16 source under
.BR cesu ),
the first does not carry
.BR fail ,
and the second has no lone-surrogate token.
An encode followed by a decode
.RB ( utf8 \(-> cp\fINNN\fP \(-> utf8 )
is lossy and is never fused.
Between transfer encodings, a step through
.B raw
or
.B hex
fuses, and
.B upper()
or
.B lower()
of a
.B hex
result becomes the
.B uc
form or the plain one.
.P
Fusion changes only what runs and what the duplicate-detection
comparator sees; the compiled program, and so a user type's
stable identifier, is the program as written.
.B "hx \-b"
prints both:
.DS
.ft CW
$ hx \-b 'utf16le(latin1_to_utf8(pass))'
  ...
    1  CONVERT    convert latin1->utf8 [] premap=none
    2  CONVERT    convert utf8->utf16le [null] premap=none
    3  HALT
--- fused for execution: 3 instructions ---
    0  PUSH_VAR   0 (pass)
    1  CONVERT    convert latin1->utf16le [] premap=none
    2  HALT
.ft R
.DE
.P
The fused instruction is that of
.BR zext16() ,
and
.B upper(hex(x))
fuses to
.BR convert(x,\ \(dqraw\(dq,\ \(dqhex\(dq,\ \(dquc\(dq) .
