.TB "Decoding Examples"
.P
Undefined bytes decode as Windows' table says, whatever it says:
CP1252 0x81 is U+0081 and CP874 0xDB the private-use U+F8C1.
Control bytes decode to the controls (CP437 0x01 is U+0001, not a
glyph).
CP850 and CP858 differ only at 0xD5 (U+0131 and \[u20AC]).
Microsoft's published files leave out CP857 0xD5 and CP869 0x80.
Windows decodes 0xD5 to the private-use U+F8BB, which the strict
decode rejects, and 0x80 to the C1 fill U+0080, which it accepts.
CP864 0x25 is
.BR % ,
where Microsoft's file has U+066A.
.P
The UTF-16 decoders read a valid surrogate pair as one code point
and handle a lone surrogate, or an odd final byte, by their policy.
The three policies reproduce three runtimes: U+FFFD is .NET
.B Encoding.UTF8
and WHATWG;
.B ?
is Java's
.BR String.getBytes("UTF-8") ;
.B cesu
is Java's modified UTF-8
.RB ( writeUTF ,
JNI), including its overlong NUL.
.na
