.H 3 "The Lenient Decoders"
.P
.B fromhex()
and
.B frombase64()
are the
.B lenient
decoding, which is never null:
.BL
.LI
.BR hex :
output byte
.I i
is nib(\fIx\fP[2\fIi\fP])\(mu16 + nib(\fIx\fP[2\fIi\fP+1]),
where nib gives the value of
.BR 0 \(en 9 ,
.BR a \(en f
and
.BR A \(en F ,
and 0 for any other byte.
A final odd character is ignored.
.LI
.BR base64 :
groups of up to four characters
.I "a b c d"
are read from the start while at least two characters remain; a
single final character is ignored.
.I a
and
.I b
are always consumed;
.I c
is consumed unless it is
.B =
or the end, and likewise
.IR d .
A consumed character outside the RFC 4648 alphabet reads as \-1.
If
.I a
or
.I b
is \-1, decoding stops and keeps the bytes so far.
Otherwise write
.IR a <<2\ |\ b >>4;
then, if
.I c
\(>= 0, write
.RI ( b &15)<<4\ |\ c >>2;
then, if
.I d
\(>= 0, write
.RI ( c &3)<<6\ |\ d
(with
.I c
= \-1 that is 0xC0\ |\ \fId\fP).
An
.B =
is never consumed, so the group after one starts on it and stops.
.LE
.na
