.TB "Lenient Decoding"
.H 3 "Functions That Are Not Conversions"
.P
These are ordinary function calls, not forms of
.BR convert() :
.BL
.LI
.B ebcdic()
is not a code page (Section 4.2.1).
.LI
.BR ucut() ,
.B utrunc()
and
.B ulength()
take or return integers.
.LI
The scheme-specific encoders
.RB ( juniper_encode() ,
.BR cisco_pix_encode() ,
.BR sha512crypt_encode() ,
.BR bcrypt_encode() ,
.BR lotus64_encode() ,
.BR progress_encode() ,
.B dahua_encode()
and
.BR besder_encode() )
permute, truncate or interleave bytes, or carry literals, so none
of them is a transfer encoding.
.LI
The output-role suffixes
.RB ( _hex ,
.BR _uc ,
.BR _b64 )
are properties of the hash call (Section 2.5.1).
.LE
.H 2 "Windows Behavior and Library Behavior"
.P
Every
.BI cp NNN
conversion reproduces Windows, not a conversion library.
Where the two disagree, hx follows Windows, because a Windows
program that converted a password produced Windows' bytes.
Each rule in this section was established on Windows 11
(build 26200, ANSI code page 1252, OEM code page 437) by calling the
Windows functions named, and each
.BI cp NNN
table is an exhaustive dump of
.B MultiByteToWideChar
and
.B WideCharToMultiByte
(Section 4.2.1).
.H 3 "Best Fit"
.P
.B WideCharToMultiByte
by default replaces a character the page lacks with a look-alike:
the best fit.
That is what every Unicode Windows program did when it passed a
password through an ANSI or OEM code page.
.B bestfit
selects it;
.BR strict ,
the default, accepts exact mappings only, as
.B WC_NO_BEST_FIT_CHARS
does, and writes the default character
.B ?
otherwise.
Best fit is locale-specific: in CP932 \(lq\[u00A5]\(rq best-fits to
0x5C, the backslash, because on Japanese Windows that byte is the
yen sign, and CP949 does the same with U+20A9 (won sign).
