In the double-byte pages one character can have two byte forms:
CP932 decodes both 0x81BE and 0x879C to \(lq\[u222A]\(rq, and
encoding chooses 0x81BE, as Windows' table does.
.H 3 "DBCS Invalid Trail Bytes"
.P
On a double-byte page, a lead byte followed by any byte other than
NUL is consumed as a pair, both bytes.
If the pair is not in the page, it decodes as the page's Unicode
default character: U+30FB (katakana middle dot) for 932,
.B ?
for 936, 949 and 950.
A lead byte followed by NUL, or at the end of the input, decodes
as the default character on its own, and the NUL is then decoded
normally.
This is
.B MultiByteToWideChar
without
.BR MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS ,
the default reading.
Under
.B null
the decode is
.B MultiByteToWideChar
with
.BR MB_ERR_INVALID_CHARS :
an invalid pair, or a lead byte at the end or before NUL, makes the
whole result null.
The libraries do otherwise: Java
.BR windows-31j ,
ICU and WHATWG
.BR shift_jis ,
and Python and Perl
.B cp932
keep the trail byte and emit U+FFFD.
The standard and
.B _web
multi-byte charsets follow the Encoding Standard's error rule
(Section 4.2.1).
