.H 2 "Unicode"
.P
Every table derived from Unicode is generated from the Unicode
18.0.0 data files and is never regenerated from a later version.
A later version can add a case mapping to a character that already
exists, when its partner is newly encoded: Unicode 11 gave 46
existing Georgian letters (U+10D0 \&...) uppercase mappings to the
new Mtavruli, Unicode 8 gave 87 existing characters mappings (the
Cherokee letters U+13A0 \&... gained lowercase partners), and
Unicode 5.0 changed U+0241's lowercase from U+0294 to U+0242.
Moving the version would therefore change the digests of
passwords containing such characters.
A token or function name always denotes the Unicode 18.0.0
table; a table from another version has its own name.
.B "hx \-V"
reports the Unicode version and where the conversion tables come
from:
.DS
.ft CW
.ps 8
$ hx \-V
hx (v0.1)
Unicode 18.0.0, frozen
cpNNN code pages, Windows case tables and LM preparation: Windows 11
KOI8-R and KOI8-U: RFC 1489 and RFC 2319
iso8859_N: ISO/IEC 8859; mac_* and adobe_symbol: Apple; adobe_standard: Adobe
jis_x0201: JIS X 0201; kz1048: STRK1048-2002; viscii: RFC 1456; hp_roman8, dec_mcs: RFC 1345
atascii, petscii, petscii_shifted: Unicode L2/19-025; ascii: ANSI X3.4
shift_jis, euc_jp, iso2022jp: JIS X 0208, JIS X 0212, RFC 1468; euc_kr: KS X 1001;
    euc_jp_c1, euc_kr_c1: with C1, as glibc
gb2312, gb18030: GB 18030-2022; *_web: WHATWG Encoding Standard
utf32*, utf16, cesu8, utf7: Unicode, UTR #26, RFC 2152; gsm0338: 3GPP TS 23.038;
    t61: ITU-T T.61
.ps
.ft R
.DE
.P
Each source is printed on one line; the two longest are wrapped
here.
.H 3 "Case Mapping"
.P
hx offers three case mappings over characters, each matching a
family of runtimes:
.BL
.LI
.I simple
.RB ( ulower() ,
.BR uupper() ,
premaps
.B lower
and
.BR upper ):
one character to one character, as Java's
.BR Character.toLowerCase(int) ;
Windows'
.B LCMapStringEx
with
.B LCMAP_LINGUISTIC_CASING
agrees except on the case pairs that Unicode 8 and 11 added
(Section 4.5.4);
.LI
.I full
.RB ( ulower_full() ,
.BR uupper_full() ,
premaps
.B lower_full
and
.BR upper_full ):
as Java's
.BR String.toLowerCase ,
JavaScript, Python's
.B str.lower
and PHP's
.BR mb_strtolower ;
.LI
.I Windows
.RB ( ulower_win() ,
.BR uupper_win() ):
Windows' standard table (Section 4.5.4).
.LE
.P
The simple mapping is UnicodeData.txt field 13 for lowercase and field 12
for uppercase.
A character with no mapping, and an ill-formed byte, is unchanged.
A simple mapping never changes the number of characters, so
truncation before and after it agree, though it can change the
byte length (U+1E9E, three bytes, lowercases to
\(lq\[u00DF]\(rq, two).
No language tailoring is applied: Turkish and Azeri dotted and
dotless I are not special, and there is no final-sigma rule.
.na
