.H 3 "Normalization"
.P
.BR nfc() ,
.BR nfd() ,
.B nfkc()
and
.B nfkd()
are the four normalization forms of UAX #15, from the Unicode
18.0.0 data.
The same visible text can arrive as different code points:
\(lq\[u00E9]\(rq is U+00E9 composed or
.B e
plus U+0301 decomposed, and a Hangul syllable is three bytes
composed and six or nine decomposed.
NFKC and NFKD also fold compatibility characters, such as
fullwidth input and ligatures.
Ill-formed bytes pass through unchanged and split the input into
separately normalized runs; normalization never crosses one.
.P
.B nfd_hfs()
is the decomposition an HFS+ volume applies to a file name, as
measured on a real volume: canonical decomposition and ordering of
combining marks only for characters assigned by Unicode 3.2,
with supplementary characters and the ranges U+2000\(enU+2FFF and
U+F900\(enU+FAFF left alone, and U+FFFE and U+FFFF written as the
text
.B %EF%BF%BE
and
.BR %EF%BF%BF .
It is not
.BR nfd() :
no supplementary character is decomposed, and later characters
are not either.
HFS+ also escapes ill-formed bytes as
.BI % XX\fR;
.B nfd_hfs()
does not, because it describes a name's text, and an ill-formed
byte passes through it unchanged.
.na
.TS
