.H 2 "Text Programs"
.P
A text program tries a password's text across many encodings, or
chains of encodings, and reports the readings that succeed.
One definition serves two purposes: a search, which generates
candidates, and a description, which names the exact conversion a
site applied.
Text programs run on the CPU only.
.H 3 "Iterating over Encodings"
.DS
.ft CW
for \fIv\fP in ("cp1252", "cp1251", "koi8r") { \fIbody\fP }
for \fIv\fP in charsets("\fIgroup\fP") { \fIbody\fP }
.ft R
.DE
.P
The loop is unrolled at compile time.
Each list item is a string literal naming a registry entry, and an
unknown name is a compile-time error.
Inside the body
.I v
is a compile-time string, and may appear only as an encoding
operand of
.BR convert() ,
an encoding of
.B valid()
or
.BR roundtrip() ,
or an
.B emit()
label.
Every
.B convert()
in the unrolled program therefore has literal operands, and the
rules of fusion and identity (Section 4.3.5) hold for it.
Nested loops give chains of conversions.
Inside braces a newline is whitespace, and statements are separated
by
.BR ; .
.P
.B charsets()
names one of eight groups:
.na
.TS
box, tab(|);
lB lB
lf(CW) lw(4.8i).
